ype":"message","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"The equality is **not established as written**, because the operation \\((f\\to g)^c\\) is not defined. It is not standard Bell-polynomial notation.\n\nIf it means replacing \\(c\\) derivative **factors** by those of \\(g\\), the complementary count is \\(k-c\\), not \\(n-c\\). Indeed, the partial Bell polynomial\n\\[\nB_{n,k}(z_1,\\ldots,z_{n-k+1})\n=\\sum_{\\substack{\\sum_j m_j=k\\\\ \\sum_j jm_j=n}}\n\\frac{n!}{\\prod_j m_j!(j!)^{m_j}}\\prod_j z_j^{m_j}\n\\]\nis homogeneous of ordinary degree \\(k\\). Writing \\(F=(f',f'',\\ldots)\\), \\(G=(g',g'',\\ldots)\\), and interpreting replacement by coefficient extraction gives\n\\[\n[t^c]B_{n,k}(F+tG)\n=[t^{k-c}]B_{n,k}(G+tF),\n\\qquad 0\\le c\\le k.\n\\]\n\nThe proposed \\(n-c\\) version fails under this interpretation. Take \\(n=3,\\ k=2,\\ c=1,\\ f(x)=x,\\ g(x)=x^2\\). Since \\(B_{3,2}(z_1,z_2)=3z_1z_2\\),\n\\[\n[t]\\,3(1+2xt)(2t)=6,\n\\qquad\n[t^2]\\,3(2x+t)(2)=0.\n\\]\nThus the two sides differ.\n\nFinal answer: The operation needs a definition; under the natural factor-replacement interpretation, the stated symmetry is false, and the correct complementary exponent is \\(k-c\\)."}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","mode":"standard","summary":null},"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":"default","store":false,"temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_logprobs":0,"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":218